From cbbba499a8507ab75a3bef8697b9c8279bca7452 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: give DomA a Venus ICD the loader can find, and
 composite on it

Before this change the guest ran on the Venus device line and composited
with GL, and the reason was not visible from either side. Measured on
the built image, and on that image booted as DomA on a PC against the
same device line:

  obj/MESON_MESA3D/.../intro-buildoptions.json
                                       vulkan-drivers = []
  install/usr/local/lib/               libEGL, libGLESv2, libgbm_mesa;
                                       no libvulkan_*
  apex/com.google.cf.vulkan/lib64/hw/  vulkan.pastel.so, vulkan.ranchu.so
  ro.hardware.vulkan                   empty
  pm list features | grep vulkan       empty

So there was no Venus ICD in the image, nothing told the loader to look
for one, and nothing asked SurfaceFlinger for Vulkan. The device-line
difference was real -- the guest DRM driver reports +resource_blob
+host_visible on the Venus lane and -resource_blob -host_visible without
it -- but every frame was still drawn by GL, and the resulting deltas
sat at run-to-run noise on the PC (totalFrames +1.6%, averageFPS
+2.0%), which reads as "Venus does nothing" rather than as a switch
nobody threw.

Six settings, and each one is load-bearing on its own.

BOARD_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVERS := virtio puts -Dvulkan-drivers=virtio on
the meson line and makes Mesa's Android.mk define a vulkan.virtio module
whose install path is /vendor/lib64/hw/vulkan.virtio.so. BoardConfig.mk
also records a build-system trap: the MESON_MESA3D rule does not list
this variable among its prerequisites, and a stale tree ships a 0-byte
ICD while the build still passes, so obj/MESON_MESA3D* has to be
removed before a rebuild is relied on.

PRODUCT_PACKAGES += vulkan.virtio installs it. Defining a module is not
installing it, and the gap is silent: a build with the board config
alone produced libvulkan_virtio.so (9.3 MB) under
obj/MESON_MESA3D/install and listed vulkan.virtio in module-info.json
with the correct install path, while /vendor/lib64/hw/ carried no
vulkan.*.so at all.

ro.hardware.vulkan=virtio is what makes the loader look for a driver by
that name at all.

TARGET_VULKAN_SUPPORT := false stops com.google.cf.vulkan from being
installed. This is the part that is easy to miss: that APEX's .rc sets
ro.vulkan.apex, and driver.cpp:208-221 then resolves the named APEX's
exported linker namespace and searches ONLY it, so an ICD in /vendor is
invisible while the property is set. The alternative -- ship the Venus
ICD inside a vendor APEX with everything it links against -- is the
much larger change. The guard covers only that APEX and SwiftShader's
execmem/use_vulkan_pastel config; the minigbm gralloc and both
hwcomposer APEXes sit outside it and are unchanged.

TARGET_VULKAN_SUPPORT is assigned ABOVE the inherit, the opposite of
the PRODUCT_* assignments below it. cuttlefish and trout both write it
with ?=, and inherit-product only pushes a marker where it appears --
the real include happens later, in node_fns.mk -- so an assignment here
pre-empts both. The comment at the top of the product makefile now
spells that out with the source line numbers, because it is easy to
get backwards.

debug.renderengine.backend=skiavkthreaded and debug.hwui.renderer=skiavk
are what actually move compositing onto Vulkan.

The android.hardware.vulkan.* feature XMLs are deliberately NOT
re-declared here. The dropped APEX shipped level-1, version-1_3 and
compute-0, which describe gfxstream rather than Venus over V3D;
re-stating them would assert a level nobody has measured on this board.
RenderEngine and HWUI reach the loader directly and do not consult
them, so compositing does not wait on them. They should follow a
vulkaninfo run on hardware.

README.md is left as it was here and in the four commits after this
one, so its Contents table and Status describe the device before the
feature files; the last commit of the series rewrites both.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [automotive-grade-linux/Android_device_sodev_xenvm-cf]
---
 aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk        | 92 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk | 32 ++++++++--
 2 files changed, 114 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk b/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
index f2dd37b..7b335c8 100644
--- a/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
+++ b/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
@@ -2,19 +2,99 @@
 #
 # Raspberry Pi 4 variant of aosp_xenvm_trout_arm64.
 #
-# Everything about the guest is inherited from the upstream product; only the identity
-# is different, so that this variant gets its own PRODUCT_DEVICE (and therefore its own
-# BoardConfig.mk and its own out/target/product/ tree). The board-specific part is in
-# xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk.
+# The guest is inherited from the upstream product and given an identity of
+# its own, so that this variant gets its own PRODUCT_DEVICE (and therefore its
+# own BoardConfig.mk and its own out/target/product/ tree). The board-specific
+# part is in xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk; what this product adds to
+# the inherited guest follows below, each block saying why it is needed.
 #
-# The inherit has to come first: the upstream makefile ends with its own PRODUCT_NAME /
-# PRODUCT_DEVICE / PRODUCT_MODEL assignments, so ours have to be written after it to win.
+# The inherit has to come first for PRODUCT_* : the upstream makefile ends with its own
+# PRODUCT_NAME / PRODUCT_DEVICE / PRODUCT_MODEL assignments, so ours have to be written
+# after it to win. The block below is the exact opposite and has to come BEFORE, for a
+# reason worth spelling out because it is easy to get backwards:
+#
+#   $(call inherit-product) does not include anything at the point it appears. It only
+#   pushes an $(INHERIT_TAG)<path> marker onto every product variable
+#   (build/make/core/product.mk:532-546); the real include happens later in
+#   build/make/core/node_fns.mk:183-207, where _import-node does `include <parent>` at
+#   :190 and only then recurses into that parent's own inherits at :201. Evaluation
+#   order is therefore leaf -> epam -> trout -> cuttlefish. Anything a parent guards
+#   with ?= or ifndef can be pre-empted by assigning it here, above the inherit.
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Vulkan: hand the loader a Venus ICD it can actually find.
+#
+# This has to be above the inherit, and it is not a style choice. cuttlefish's
+# shared/device.mk:46 and trout's aosp_trout_common.mk:97 both write
+# TARGET_VULKAN_SUPPORT ?= true, and evaluation order is leaf -> epam -> trout
+# -> cuttlefish (see the note at the top of this file), so an assignment here
+# pre-empts both.
+#
+# WHY DROP IT. com.google.cf.vulkan is the APEX that carries gfxstream's
+# vulkan.ranchu and SwiftShader's vulkan.pastel, and its own .rc sets
+# ro.vulkan.apex=com.google.cf.vulkan. That property is not a hint:
+# frameworks/native/vulkan/libvulkan/driver.cpp:208-221 resolves the named
+# APEX's exported linker namespace and passes it to LoadDriver, so the loader
+# searches ONLY that namespace. A Venus ICD installed at
+# /vendor/lib64/hw/vulkan.virtio.so is therefore invisible while the property
+# is set, and the failure is the namespace one -- the APEX namespace has no
+# libui.so -- not a missing file. The two ways out are to ship the Venus ICD
+# inside a vendor APEX with everything it links against, or to stop using the
+# APEX driver path; this is the second, and it is much the smaller change.
+#
+# What false removes is narrow, and the scope is measured, not assumed:
+# `grep -rn TARGET_VULKAN_SUPPORT device/google/trout device/google/cuttlefish
+# device/epam` finds exactly two `ifeq` sites in the tree, both in cuttlefish.
+# They are PRODUCT_PACKAGES += com.google.cf.vulkan
+# (cuttlefish/shared/graphics/device_vendor.mk:45) and SwiftShader's
+# PRODUCT_REQUIRES_INSECURE_EXECMEM_FOR_SWIFTSHADER plus
+# soong_config_set cvd use_vulkan_pastel
+# (cuttlefish/shared/swiftshader/device_vendor.mk:17). trout only assigns
+# the variable, never tests it, and epam does not mention it. The minigbm
+# gralloc (com.google.cf.gralloc) and both hwcomposer APEXes are outside the
+# guard and are unaffected.
+#
+# NOT DONE HERE, deliberately: the android.hardware.vulkan.* feature XMLs. The
+# dropped APEX shipped level-1, version-1_3 and compute-0 as prebuilts. Those
+# levels describe gfxstream, not Venus over V3D, and re-declaring them would be
+# asserting a capability nobody measured on this board. SurfaceFlinger's
+# RenderEngine and HWUI do not consult them -- they go through the loader -- so
+# Vulkan compositing does not wait on this. Declare them from a vulkaninfo run
+# on the board, one level at a time.
+TARGET_VULKAN_SUPPORT := false
+
 $(call inherit-product, device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout/aosp_xenvm_trout_arm64.mk)
 
 PRODUCT_NAME := aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64
 PRODUCT_DEVICE := xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64
 PRODUCT_MODEL := xenvm arm64 trout (Raspberry Pi 4)
 
+# Install the ICD. BOARD_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVERS makes Mesa's Android.mk DEFINE
+# the module -- android/Android.mk:203-205 expands it to vulkan.virtio with an
+# install path of /vendor/lib64/hw/vulkan.virtio.so -- but a defined module that
+# no product asks for is never installed. Measured on a full build with the
+# board config alone: meson produced libvulkan_virtio.so (9.3 MB) in
+# obj/MESON_MESA3D/install, module-info.json listed vulkan.virtio with the right
+# install path, and /vendor/lib64/hw/ carried no vulkan.*.so at all.
+PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
+    vulkan.virtio
+
+# Vulkan, guest side. ro.hardware.vulkan is what makes the loader look for
+# vulkan.<name>.so at all -- with it empty, as it was before this change, the
+# loader searches for nothing and pm list features reports no Vulkan.
+# BOARD_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVERS in this device's BoardConfig.mk is what builds
+# the matching ICD.
+PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
+    ro.hardware.vulkan=virtio
+
+# What actually turns Vulkan compositing on. Without these two the guest runs
+# on the Venus device line and still composites with GL, which is measurable
+# only as run-to-run noise -- so their absence looks like "Venus does nothing"
+# rather than like a missing switch.
+PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
+    debug.renderengine.backend=skiavkthreaded \
+    debug.hwui.renderer=skiavk
+
 # Start the interface rename that minradio's data call depends on. cuttlefish already
 # ships the service and a trigger for it, but trout sets ro.vendor.disable_rename_eth0
 # so that trigger never fires; overriding that is deliberate and argued in the .rc.
diff --git a/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
index 9893b87..3443306 100644
--- a/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
+++ b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
@@ -2,10 +2,11 @@
 #
 # Board configuration for the Raspberry Pi 4 variant of the xenvm-trout AAOS guest.
 #
-# The upstream board config is included wholesale and only the ISA baseline is changed
-# after it, so this file stays a delta rather than a fork: partition sizes, bootconfig,
-# sepolicy dirs, kernel cmdline and the mesa3d/virgl selection all keep coming from
-# device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout.
+# The upstream board config is included wholesale, and everything after it adds
+# to it: the ISA baseline, then one section for each DomA feature that needs a
+# board variable. This file stays a delta rather than a fork -- partition sizes,
+# bootconfig, the kernel cmdline, epam's sepolicy dirs and the Gallium (virgl)
+# driver selection all keep coming from device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout.
 include device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout/xenvm_trout_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
 
 # --- Raspberry Pi 4 (BCM2711) ISA baseline ---------------------------------------
@@ -49,3 +50,26 @@ include device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout/xenvm_trout_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
 # raspberrypi4-64 and are therefore already safe.
 TARGET_ARCH_VARIANT := armv8-a
 TARGET_CPU_VARIANT := cortex-a72
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Build the Venus ICD.
+#
+# The included board config sets BOARD_MESA3D_USES_MESON_BUILD and
+# GALLIUM_DRIVERS := virgl and stops there, so the product's Mesa was built
+# with -Dvulkan-drivers= and nothing after it: measured in
+# obj/MESON_MESA3D/.../intro-buildoptions.json as vulkan-drivers = [], and the
+# install tree carries libEGL/libGLESv2/libgbm_mesa and no libvulkan_*.so at
+# all. Naming the driver here is what puts -Dvulkan-drivers=virtio on the meson
+# line (vendor/epam/mesa3d_upstream/android/mesa3d_cross.mk:92) and what makes
+# android/Android.mk:203-205 emit the vulkan.virtio module into
+# /vendor/lib64/hw/.
+#
+# One build-system trap worth knowing before touching this: the MESON_MESA3D
+# .build.timestamp rule does rm -rf / cp -r / meson setup / ninja wholesale, and
+# its prerequisites do not include this variable, so a change here is not
+# guaranteed to retrigger it by make semantics alone. Worse,
+# mesa3d_cross.mk's vulkan_target touches an empty .so when the real one is
+# absent and Android.mk's mesa3d-lib sets LOCAL_CHECK_ELF_FILES := false, so a
+# stale tree ships a 0-byte ICD and the build still passes. Remove
+# obj/MESON_MESA3D* before relying on a rebuild.
+BOARD_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVERS := virtio
